const article: BlogArticle = {"slug": "ai-short-drama-workflow", "oldSlugs": [], "column": "guide", "category": "guide", "title": "AI Short Drama Workflow: Script to Vertical Episode", "seoTitle": "AI Short Drama Workflow: Vertical Episodes | DramaCanvas", "summary": "Producing a 90-second AI short drama episode on DramaCanvas takes 10 to 15 shots and costs about $17 to $52 in generation credits at 720p. This step-by-step tutorial walks through cliffhanger scripting, character consistency, video generation, and audio assembly on an infinite canvas.", "author": "", "date": "2026-11-04", "modifiedDate": "2026-11-04", "updated": "2026-11-04", "status": "published", "seoApproved": true, "sourceLocale": "en-US", "publishedLocales": ["en-US"], "intro": ["An **ai short drama** is a scripted narrative series produced with generative image and video models, engineered specifically for vertical smartphone screens. Each episode runs between 60 and 120 seconds, opening on an immediate visual hook and cutting to black on an unresolved cliffhanger. In China, where the format originated and exploded into a multibillion-dollar industry, the genre is called duanju.", "According to Sensor Tower's June 2026 data reported by [ScreenWeaver](https://www.screenweaver.ai/blog/ai-short-drama), global in-app revenue across short drama apps surged 20% year over year to approximately US$750 million in Q1 2026 alone, driven by more than 850 million installs. Viewers on platforms like ReelShort and DramaBox consume an average of 25 minutes of vertical content per day. Solo creators can now produce full vertical pilots without production crews, physical sets, or studio cameras.", "This comprehensive guide is Part 1 of our 6-part production series. By following this 30-minute walkthrough, you will learn how to structure cliffhanger scripts, maintain strict character consistency, stage multi-model video shots, synthesize dialogue, and assemble a 90-second vertical episode on the DramaCanvas [[link:infinite-canvas-guide|infinite canvas]]."], "takeaways": ["**Plan 10 to 15 shots per 90-second episode**: Micro dramas require rapid visual pacing, cutting between medium shots and dialogue close-ups every 3 to 6 seconds.", "**Lock one master portrait before generating video**: Character drift ruins narrative immersion, so you must establish front and side reference stills before wiring assets downstream.", "**Mix models to control cost**: A 90-second 720p episode costs 630 credits ($17.01) on Kling 4 alone and 1,944 credits ($52.49) with [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]] close-ups plus [[link:wan-3-guide|Wan 3.0]] for the rest.", "**Board layouts consume zero generation credits**: Staging cards, drafting scripts, and connecting wires on DramaCanvas are completely free until you submit generation tasks."], "questions": [{"id": "question-1", "title": "What is an AI short drama and why is the format expanding?"}, {"id": "question-2", "title": "How do you write a vertical script built around cliffhangers?"}, {"id": "question-3", "title": "How does the Storyboard script node break outlines into shot rows?"}, {"id": "question-4", "title": "How do you lock character cards and references for visual consistency?"}, {"id": "question-5", "title": "How do you generate 9:16 keyframes and animate them into video shots?"}, {"id": "question-6", "title": "Which video model should you choose for each shot type on DramaCanvas?"}, {"id": "question-7", "title": "How do you synthesize dialogue and voiceover with the Audio node?"}, {"id": "question-8", "title": "How much does one 90-second AI short drama episode cost?"}, {"id": "question-9", "title": "When is the infinite canvas the wrong tool for your production?"}], "sections": [{"id": "question-1", "title": "What is an AI short drama and why is the format expanding?", "paragraphs": ["An AI short drama is a serialized narrative formatted in 9:16 vertical video that generated $750 million in global in-app revenue in Q1 2026. Unlike traditional horizontal cinema or casual social video clips, a short drama (also known as a vertical drama or micro drama) delivers a complete narrative arc across 40 to 100 tightly linked episodes. The format relies heavily on emotional high-concept engines such as hidden heirs, corporate betrayal, revenge contracts, and secret identities.", "The format aligns naturally with current AI generative model constraints. Vertical dramas eliminate these liabilities by framing scenes primarily in medium close-ups, focusing intently on two speaking actors, and cutting every 2 to 5 seconds.", "Mobile audiences also watch short drama series with higher retention than open-ended video feeds. As noted by [OpenMicroDrama](https://openmicrodrama.com/guides/how-to-make-an-ai-micro-drama), independent studio Spirit Studios described the vertical format to Deadline as roughly 60 episodes of 90 seconds, building in 60 consecutive cliffhangers. Because viewers decide whether to keep watching within the first three seconds, every episode must present an immediate visual disruption before demanding emotional investment."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}]}, {"id": "question-2", "title": "How do you write a vertical script built around cliffhangers?", "paragraphs": ["A 90-second vertical drama script requires 10 to 15 distinct shots, no more than ten lines of dialogue, and a sharp cliffhanger delivered in the final three seconds. Writing for vertical video differs fundamentally from standard feature screenplays. You must avoid ambient scene-setting, scenic transitions, and multi-character group conversations that crowd smartphone screens.", "A reliable episode structure has four beats: Hook, Stakes, Turn and Cliffhanger. The Hook halts the viewer's scroll within the first three seconds with an unexpected visual action. The Stakes establish what the protagonist wants and the obstacle blocking them. The Turn reveals sudden information that upends the balance of power, while the Cliffhanger cuts the action one beat before the resolution.", "Before generating any visual assets, compile a concise series bible containing your central logline, three recurring locations, and detailed character visual profiles. Keeping dialogue lean is critical because every spoken word requires precise audio generation and lip-sync alignment downstream. Use the prompt formula below inside a Text node on DramaCanvas to draft your episode beat sheet."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "code", "index": 0}], "code": [{"label": "Vertical drama episode 1 beat prompt", "content": "Write a 90-second vertical 9:16 micro drama script for Episode 1 of an urban suspense series.\nProtagonist: Elena Vance (28, investigative reporter in a charcoal trench coat).\nSetting: Dimly lit archive office at midnight, rain drumming on exterior windows.\nBeat 1 (0-15s Hook): Elena unlocks a private safe, revealing an envelope marked with her father's case number.\nBeat 2 (15-45s Stakes): She flips through bank transfers linking the CEO to an offshore shell company.\nBeat 3 (45-75s Turn): A shadow crosses the frosted door; heavy footsteps halt right outside.\nBeat 4 (75-90s Cliffhanger): The door handle turns slowly downwards. A text notification dings on her phone: \"Look under the desk.\"\nFormat: 12 numbered shots with shot type, visible character action, framing, and concise dialogue.", "language": "text"}]}, {"id": "question-3", "title": "How does the Storyboard script node break outlines into shot rows?", "paragraphs": ["The DramaCanvas Storyboard script node is a 920×360 table card that uses text models to automatically break story outlines into sequential shot rows with durations between 1 and 60 seconds. Rather than manually copying prompts into separate video cards, this node structures your entire episode script inside an interactive production table. Each row includes dedicated columns for shot number, duration, camera motion prompt, spoken dialogue, and reference media.", "**Step 1: Add the Storyboard script node and input your premise**", "Click **Add node** on the bottom dock toolbar and select **Storyboard script**. In the outline input box along the bottom edge of the card, paste your written episode beats or scene outline. Select your preferred text reasoning model from the model picker dropdown, then click **Generate storyboard** (or press `Ctrl/⌘+Enter`).", "The table automatically populates with sequential shot rows configured with default 6-second durations. You can adjust individual shot lengths between 1 and 60 seconds, click **Add row** to insert intermediate inserts, or select **Delete shot** to prune redundant frames. Click **Edit full screen** whenever you need an expanded view to refine dialogue lines or camera directions.", "**Step 2: Generate keyframe stills and video shots across rows**", "In the Storyboard script card menu, click **Generate unfinished storyboard images** to render reference stills for every row simultaneously. This batch process lets you audit visual continuity, costume colors, and lighting setups across your entire narrative sequence before committing video credits. If any still drifts from your visual bible, edit that specific row's prompt and regenerate that single image.", "Once your keyframe images match your creative vision, click **Generate unfinished videos** in the card menu to render motion clips for all staged rows. When at least two video clips finish rendering, the primary **Merge videos** button unlocks on the card header. Clicking it compiles your individual shots into a continuous vertical drama cut. In Professional mode, you can also click **Create image nodes** or **Create video nodes** to split table rows into standalone canvas cards for granular fine-tuning."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "figure", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}], "figures": [{"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-01-storyboard.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-01-storyboard.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 2099, "alt": "Sequential storyboard table for the archive mystery with default 6s shots", "caption": "Sequential storyboard table for the archive mystery with default 6s shots"}]}, {"id": "question-4", "title": "How do you lock character cards and references for visual consistency?", "paragraphs": ["Preventing character drift across an AI series requires locking one primary portrait image and a fixed 4-part physical description before rendering any video. Face drift occurs when generative models interpret vague adjectives differently across shots, creating an inconsistent lead that breaks narrative immersion. As outlined in [ScreenWeaver's production guide](https://www.screenweaver.ai/blog/ai-short-drama), successful creators build locked reference sheets for all recurring leads prior to generating shot 1.", "**Step 1: Draft an unambiguous character description**", "In a Text node, draft a four-part visual anchor: age, facial structure, distinctive hair, and signature wardrobe. Avoid generic descriptors like \"handsome\" or \"stunning\" that force models to invent random features. Give your character one distinctive physical anchor—such as a specific silver pendant, a faint jawline scar, or tortoiseshell reading glasses—that models can latch onto consistently.", "**Step 2: Generate and pin the master portrait card**", "Connect your Text node to an Image node set to 9:16 vertical resolution using [[link:gpt-image-guide-2026|GPT Image]] or [[link:nano-banana-pro|Nano Banana]]. Generate front-facing, three-quarter, and profile portraits in neutral, even lighting. Dramatic cinematic lighting should be avoided in reference cards because video models will inherit the shadow geometry into subsequent daylight scenes.", "Once you approve the master portrait, treat that Image card as your character anchor. On DramaCanvas, you can wire that master Image card into multiple downstream Video nodes simultaneously. The canvas automatically deduplicates identical source connections, meaning routing the same character reference multiple times consumes only one reference slot on downstream cards."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "code", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}], "code": [{"label": "Lead character master description prompt", "content": "Elena Vance, 28-year-old investigative journalist. Pale oval face, sharp cheekbones, almond-shaped hazel eyes with focused expression. Shoulder-length dark auburn hair parted on the left with subtle waves. Wardrobe: Tailored charcoal wool trench coat over a high-collar black knit turtleneck, wearing a delicate silver geometric pendant necklace. Cinematic studio lighting, neutral background, photorealistic texture.", "language": "text"}]}, {"id": "question-5", "title": "How do you generate 9:16 keyframes and animate them into video shots?", "paragraphs": ["Creating an animated shot on DramaCanvas requires connecting a 9:16 Image node directly to a Video node's left input port and selecting the image in the Start frame dropdown. Animating a pre-approved keyframe still (image-to-video) ensures your composition, character placement, and lighting remain locked. Text-to-video generation leaves spatial blocking to chance, leading to frequent retries that consume unnecessary credits.", "**Step 1: Set up the keyframe Image node**", "Click **Add node** on the bottom dock toolbar and select **Image**. Click **Generation settings** beneath the prompt box to open the Image settings modal. Set **Size** to 9:16 vertical, select **Quality** as high, and ensure generation count is set to 1. Connect your master character card into the left input port of this Image node as an upstream reference.", "Paste your shot description into the input box and submit generation by clicking the upward arrow icon. Review the resulting still carefully to confirm hand placement, facial expression, and background perspective. If small details require adjustment, use the **Inpaint** tool on the card toolbar to brush over imperfections rather than regenerating the entire card.", "**Step 2: Wire the Image node to the Video node**", "Click **Add node** and select **Video**, placing the card to the right of your keyframe Image card. Drag a wire from the circular output port on the right edge of your Image node into the circular input port on the left edge of your Video node. Once linked, the keyframe thumbnail appears inside the Video node's parameter tray.", "Click the settings summary under the input box (such as \"720P · 16:9 · 5s\") to configure video parameters. Set **Resolution** to 720P, choose 9:16 vertical under **Size**, and select your target clip duration (such as 5 or 6 seconds). Crucially, expand **Parameters** beneath the prompt input, click the **Start frame** dropdown menu, and select your connected keyframe still to lock it as the opening state. Leave **End frame** set to \"Not specified\" unless you are intentionally connecting a second image to bridge two distinct camera states.", "**Step 3: Submit generation and manage canvas cards**", "Check the credit cost displayed directly on the generate button, then click the upward arrow to queue the task. The card enters the Queued phase before displaying a real-time progress bar. Once rendering completes, preview the MP4 video directly within the card's native player. Group your Text, Image, and Video nodes together by dropping a **Frame** container around them, double-clicking the header to label it \"Shot 1: Archive Encounter,\" and folding it to keep your workspace clear."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "figure", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "code", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "figure", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}, {"type": "figure", "index": 2}, {"type": "code", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 7}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 8}], "figures": [{"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-02-image-settings.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-02-image-settings.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 1412, "alt": "Elena archive keyframe configured with 9:16 vertical Size, high Quality and 1 image", "caption": "Elena archive keyframe configured with 9:16 vertical Size, high Quality and 1 image"}, {"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-03-references.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-03-references.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 2520, "alt": "Wiring the keyframe Image node output port to the Video node input port", "caption": "Wiring the keyframe Image node output port to the Video node input port"}, {"src": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-04-settings.jpg", "webp": "/blog-media/figures/ai-short-drama-workflow/fig-04-settings.webp", "width": 1920, "height": 2978, "alt": "Setting Seedance 2.5 to 720P, 9:16 and 6s with credit cost displayed", "caption": "Setting Seedance 2.5 to 720P, 9:16 and 6s with credit cost displayed"}], "code": [{"label": "Shot 1 keyframe first-frame prompt", "content": "9:16 vertical medium close-up shot. Elena Vance stands inside a dark corporate archive office at midnight. Cold blue ambient rain light washes through venetian blinds onto her profile. She looks down tensely toward a brass safe door standing slightly ajar, right hand poised near the lock. Charcoal trench coat, dark auburn hair falling forward over her collar.", "language": "text"}, {"label": "Shot 1 camera motion video prompt", "content": "9:16 vertical camera trajectory. Smooth slow push-in toward Elena's face, continuing seamlessly from the start frame. Raindrops trickle down the dark window glass in the soft background bokeh. Elena holds her breath as her hazel eyes dart from the opened safe toward the corridor door outside. Subtle breathing motion in her shoulders under cold fluorescent lighting.", "language": "text"}]}, {"id": "question-6", "title": "Which video model should you choose for each shot type on DramaCanvas?", "paragraphs": ["ByteDance's Seedance 2.5 delivers high-fidelity close-ups supporting up to 30 reference images at 30 credits per second, while Kling 4 provides dynamic camera motion at 7 credits per second in 720p. Matching specific shot requirements to the appropriate video model is the most effective way to protect your production budget while preserving visual quality. A single 90-second episode should combine multiple generative models based on shot complexity.", "Dialogue scenes and emotional close-ups require strong facial coherence. [[link:seedance-2-5|Seedance 2.5]] and [[link:seedance-2|Seedance 2.0]] excel in these setups due to their extensive multi-image reference inputs, allowing you to feed multiple costume angles and facial close-ups simultaneously. Conversely, dynamic action sequences, object inserts, and rapid establishing pans are handled efficiently by [[link:kling-ai-guide-2026|Kling 4]] or [[link:wan-3-guide|Wan 3.0]] at a fraction of the per-second credit cost.", "Note that OpenAI shut down the Sora 2 models and Videos API on 2026-09-24 (following the closure of the Sora web and app interfaces on 2026-04-26). If you are transitioning legacy prompts from that architecture, consult our index of [[link:sora-shutdown-alternatives|Sora alternatives]] to remap parameters to current production engines. Review the verified model table below to select the right generator for each node on your board.", "| Model (maker) | Resolution & credits per second | Length | Aspect ratios | Inputs on DramaCanvas | Best use case & limitations | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Seedance 2.5 (ByteDance) | 480p: 14 · 720p: 30 · 1080p: 74 | 4–30 s | 6 ratios | Up to 30 reference images, 10 reference audio clips | Emotional dialogue close-ups; does not accept reference video on DramaCanvas | | Seedance 2.0 (ByteDance) | 480p: 10 · 720p: 20 · 1080p: 50 | 4–15 s (720p up to 30 s) | 6 ratios | Up to 9 reference images, 3 reference videos, 3 reference audio | Narrative continuity shots; high credit consumption at 1080p | | Seedance 2.0 Fast | 480p: 8 · 720p: 17 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | 9 images, 3 videos, 3 audio | Rapid scene blocking and staging checks; lower visual detail | | Seedance 2.0 Mini | 480p: 7 · 720p: 10 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | 9 images, 3 audio | Secondary character reactions; no reference video input | | Wan 3.0 (Alibaba) | 480p: 6 · 720p: 12 · 1080p: 24 | 2–30 s | 6 ratios | Up to 10 reference images, 5 reference videos, 5 reference audio | Versatile medium shots and complex lighting; moderate generation queue times | | Kling 3.0 (Kuaishou) | 720p: 8 (12 with sound) · 1080p: 16 · 4K: 40 | 3–15 s | 16:9 / 9:16 / 1:1 | 2 images as start + end frame | Native audio generation; strictly accepts start and end frames with no auxiliary video references | | MiniMax H3 (Hailuo) | 480p: 5 · 768p: 8 · 1080p: 13 | 4–30 s (1080p up to 15 s) | 6 ratios | 1 image as start frame | Economy camera pans; strictly restricted to a single start frame input | | Kling 4 (Kuaishou) | 720p: 7 · 1080p: 12 | 3–30 s | 16:9 / 9:16 / 1:1 / 21:9 | Up to 10 reference images | Dynamic physical motion and props; native sound generation unavailable | | Grok Imagine Video 1.5 (xAI) | 480p: 6 · 720p: 10 | 4–15 s | 6 ratios | Up to 7 reference images | Atmospheric environmental inserts; limited facial micro-control |", "Credits checked on DramaCanvas on 2026-11-04.", "For automated terminal pipelines, developers can also orchestrate these video generation nodes directly through the [[link:claude-video-generation-mcp|DramaCanvas MCP server]], passing script cards and wire instructions programmatically from external IDE environments."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "table", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}]}, {"id": "question-7", "title": "How do you synthesize dialogue and voiceover with the Audio node?", "paragraphs": ["The DramaCanvas Audio node generates character dialogue and room ambience at speech rates between 0.25x and 4.0x while accepting at most one voice-bound character card. Clear dialogue anchors the narrative, while subtle ambient room tones make sudden cuts feel intentional rather than amateurish.", "**Step 1: Place and configure the Audio node**", "Click **Add node** on the bottom dock toolbar and select **Audio**. The card measures 340×120 and features an interactive audio waveform display upon completion. Under parameters, select your vocal profile from the **Voice** dropdown menu (defaulting to alloy), set **Format** to mp3, and select your target playback speed using quick presets (0.75x to 1.5x) or the granular 0.25x to 4.0x slider.", "In the **Voice instructions** input field, define the actor's vocal texture and cadence using natural language. Specifying emotional subtext (such as \"Hushed, hurried whisper, breathing heavily between words\") gives better results than leaving it blank. In the prompt area, type the exact dialogue line wrapped in standard quotation marks.", "**Step 2: Understand Audio node connection rules**", "The Audio node operates under strict input constraints. It accepts either plain text prompts from Text cards or a connection from exactly one voice-bound Character card. It rejects incoming video clips, standard image cards, and auxiliary audio files; attempting to connect these incompatible media types causes the wire to snap back cleanly.", "If your character has an assigned voice profile in your project assets, wiring their character card into the Audio node locks that specific timbre across all episodes. Once generated, hover over the Audio card's toolbar to click **Download audio** or connect the clip into your multi-track timeline for final mixing against ambient background tracks."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 6}]}, {"id": "question-8", "title": "How much does one 90-second AI short drama episode cost?", "paragraphs": ["Rendering one 90-second 720p episode across 15 shots on DramaCanvas costs between 630 credits ($17.01) on an economy model mix and 1,944 credits ($52.49) on a high-fidelity mix. DramaCanvas operates on a transparent credit model tied to video duration. On the Standard paid plan ($29.90 for 1,100 credits), credits evaluate to approximately $0.027 per credit, allowing creators to accurately calculate exact shot costs before clicking generate.", "To maximize budget efficiency, production teams should structure episodes into three distinct shot tiers. Hero dialogue shots featuring primary leads should utilize models with advanced reference inputs like Seedance 2.5 or Wan 3.0. Secondary reaction shots and intermediate camera movements can be assigned to Kling 4, while rapid object inserts (such as phone screens, door locks, or clocks) should run on economy tiers.", "The budget breakdown below outlines three realistic production configurations for a standard 90-second vertical episode consisting of 15 individual 6-second shots rendered at 720p resolution.", "| Production Tier | Model Mix (720p Resolution) | Shot Breakdown (15 shots × 6s = 90s) | Total Credits | Episode Cost (USD) | Best For | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Economy Fast Draft | Kling 4 (7 credits/s) | 15 shots × 6s @ 7 credits/s | 630 credits | $17.01 | Rapid script prototyping, pacing validation, and rough-cut animatics | | Balanced Standard | Wan 3.0 (12 credits/s) + Kling 4 (7 credits/s) | 8 dialogue shots Wan 3.0 (576) + 7 action shots Kling 4 (294) | 870 credits | $23.49 | Commercial vertical series, web novel adaptations, and independent pilots | | High-Fidelity Drama | Seedance 2.5 (30 credits/s) + Wan 3.0 (12 credits/s) | 8 hero close-ups Seedance 2.5 (1,440) + 7 supporting shots Wan 3.0 (504) | 1,944 credits | $52.49 | Premium festival submissions, high-retention paywall episodes (Episodes 1–10) |", "Credits checked on DramaCanvas on 2026-11-04. Standard plan pricing calculated at $0.027 per credit ($29.90 for 1,100 credits).", "Platform credits are deducted only when you submit a task for generation. If an upstream model encounters a safety flag or network interruption, platform credits are refunded automatically to your account balance. For creators benchmarking overall production budgets against desktop tools, see our analysis of [[link:higgsfield-pricing-2026|Higgsfield pricing]] and cloud canvas alternatives."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 2}, {"type": "table", "index": 3}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 4}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 5}]}, {"id": "question-9", "title": "When is the infinite canvas the wrong tool for your production?", "paragraphs": ["The infinite canvas is the wrong tool for single one-off prompt tests or sprawling 16:9 cinematic projects that lack recurring characters and shared sets. If your immediate objective is generating a single standalone background video or testing an experimental prompt variation, opening an unstructured canvas and wiring multiple cards introduces unnecessary overhead. In those cases, generating directly inside a single standalone node without spatial staging is faster.", "Similarly, packing 50 unrelated narrative scenes onto a single workspace creates visual clutter and strains browser memory. DramaCanvas projects should be partitioned logically: assign one canvas per short drama episode or scene cluster. Grouping cards inside Frame containers keeps minimaps clean, while unlinking old drafts into project asset folders preserves system responsiveness across extended productions."], "blocks": [{"type": "paragraph", "index": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "index": 1}]}], "faq": [{"question": "Does creating, moving, and wiring cards consume generation credits?", "answer": "Zero credits are consumed. Creating canvases, dropping cards, moving layouts, connecting data wires, and grouping cards into frames are completely free. Credits are deducted strictly when you click the upward arrow to submit an Image, Video, or Audio generation task, and the exact credit cost is displayed on the button before submission."}, {"question": "Why did a connection wire snap back immediately after dragging?", "answer": "Check whether your source and destination node types are compatible. Connection ports enforce strict media type validation: dragging an audio or video wire into an Image node triggers an automatic snap-back. Wires will also snap back if the downstream node has already reached the maximum reference capacity supported by its active model."}, {"question": "Why didn't the Video node use my connected image as its opening frame?", "answer": "Open the Parameters menu below the video prompt box and select your image from the Start frame dropdown. Wiring an image into a Video node supplies it as a reference asset, but the system does not automatically designate it as the first frame unless explicitly assigned. Always confirm that your still is checked under Start frame with End frame left as Not specified."}, {"question": "How do you fix face drift across multiple episodes?", "answer": "Lock one approved master portrait card in your project assets and wire that identical Image node into every downstream video shot. Pairing this fixed visual reference with an identical four-part text description (age, facial structure, hair, signature wardrobe) prevents models from hallucinating alternative facial features. Avoid altering character costume details or hairstyles mid-series unless demanded by a specific narrative plot point."}], "media": [], "cta": "/canvas", "closing": ["Creating an impactful vertical series requires discipline, consistent visual references, and tight cliffhanger engineering. By staging your script outline inside the Storyboard script node and routing keyframes through cost-effective video models, you can produce compelling mobile episodes without expensive studio equipment.", "**Next step**: Open an empty board on the [DramaCanvas infinite canvas](https://dramacanva.com), drop a Storyboard script node to outline your pilot, and connect your first Image node to render Shot 1."], "howTo": false};
export default article;
